Which in his times he shall shew, [who is] the 

          He combines many words together for one purpose: by which he confirms the power of God, which if we trust steadfastly in, we will not be moved out of our position.
         blessed and only Potentate, the King of kings, and Lord of lords;

He adds as an overabundance as it were a sharp admonition to the rich, that they mainly take heed of two evils, that is, of pride, and deceitful hope, against which he sets three excellent virtues, hope in the living God, liberality towards their neighbour, and gentle conditions. Charge them that are rich in
In things pertaining to this life, with whom those men are compared who are rich in good works. this world, that they be not highminded, nor trust in uncertain riches, but in the
Who alone is, and that everlasting: for he sets the frail nature of riches against God. living God, who giveth us richly all things to enjoy;
